{"title":"2024 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 22","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Daily_Dose_of_Math\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_DDM\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by DDM</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_Math-X\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by Math-X</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Central_Valley_Math_Circle_(Goes_through_full_thought_process)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Central Valley Math Circle (Goes through full thought process)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_(A_Clever_Explanation_You’ll_Get_Instantly)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution (A Clever Explanation You’ll Get Instantly)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Power_Solve\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">13</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Power Solve</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-14\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2_by_OmegaLearn.org\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">14</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2 by OmegaLearn.org</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-15\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_(Arithmetic_Series)3_by_SpreadTheMathlove_Using\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">15</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution (Arithmetic Series)3 by SpreadTheMathlove Using</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-16\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_NiuniuMaths_(Easy_to_understand!)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">16</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by NiuniuMaths (Easy to understand!)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-17\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_CosineMethod_[Clever_solution_you&#39;ll_get_instantly]\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">17</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by CosineMethod [Clever solution you'll get instantly]</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-18\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Interstigation\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">18</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Interstigation</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-19\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Dr._David\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">19</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Dr. David</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-20\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_WhyMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">20</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by WhyMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-21\"><a href=\"#Official_Video_Solution_(Real-world_visualization_method-_2_minutes!)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">21</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Official Video Solution (Real-world visualization method- 2 minutes!)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-22\"><a href=\"#Video_solution_by_TheNeuralMathAcademy\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">22</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video solution by TheNeuralMathAcademy</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-23\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_SquishBJ_(AIME_Qualifier)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">23</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by SquishBJ (AIME Qualifier)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-24\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">24</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A  roll of tape is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> inches in diameter and is wrapped around a ring that is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> inches in diameter. A cross section of the tape is shown in the figure below. The tape is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/8/958af00c2d554d63afe1c50b8c2da32e1bd13abc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.015$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" /> inches thick. If the tape is completely unrolled, approximately how long would it be? Round your answer to the nearest <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/9/e59e2c6e83eb78cca610a5fd4070ae01c8d4ae60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> inches.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/f/fcfe2b58e63110a4e3c3a711bfea3cb9da7f8e7a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] /* AMC8 P22 2024, revised by Teacher David */ size(150);  pair o = (0,0); real r1 = 1; real r2 = 2;  filldraw(circle(o, r2), mediumgray, linewidth(1pt)); filldraw(circle(o, r1), white, linewidth(1pt));  draw((-2,-2.6)--(-2,-2.4)); draw((2,-2.6)--(2,-2.4)); draw((-2,-2.5)--(2,-2.5), L=Label(&quot;4 in.&quot;));  draw((-1,0)--(1,0), L=Label(&quot;2 in.&quot;, align=(0,1)), arrow=Arrows());  draw((2,0)--(2,-1.3), linewidth(1pt)); [/asy]\" width=\"205\" height=\"252\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/1/4a1964cca1ff4a369533a242c0304a7c0e97c44f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 300\\qquad\\textbf{(B) } 600\\qquad\\textbf{(C) } 1200\\qquad\\textbf{(D) } 1500\\qquad\\textbf{(E) } 1800$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"473\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The roll of tape is 1/0.015 ≈ 66 layers thick. In order to find the total length, we have to find the average of each concentric circle and multiply it by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/4/2d40f3652c1962e98ce050a88c04ed37997c67df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$66$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />. Since the diameter of the small circle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> inches and the diameter of the large one is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> inches, the \"middle value\" (or mean) is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Therefore, the average circumference is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/d/f5d4a39c601726949a5cfde4a9bbd8d30a64fb56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\pi$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" />. Multiplying <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/2/7023fcd425d10db9a63d5d7c2c61391168ea14ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\pi \\cdot 66$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"53\" height=\"14\" /> gives approximately <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/b/dab970161e56adc4a96366c373b083d07ac689f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{(B)\\ 600}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"75\" height=\"32\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There are about <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/3/ec31eec783eb616fd324e05ddeb180fb71a0e40f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{1}{0.015}=\\dfrac{200}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"103\" height=\"38\" /> \"full circles\" of tape, and with average circumference of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f3788ae4c3ef1a8b1bcf8f841a33f50b73e33f5c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{4+2}{2}\\pi=3\\pi.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"107\" height=\"38\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/9/77935eafcab3802f00da4c9e21fdda82a8ca1ebb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{200}{3} \\cdot 3\\pi=200\\pi,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"134\" height=\"38\" /> which means the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/b/15ba7407d40b78e416405cb7cdff64554a3ba647.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{600}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"39\" height=\"23\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can figure out the length of the tape by considering the side of the tape as a really thin rectangle that has a width of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/8/958af00c2d554d63afe1c50b8c2da32e1bd13abc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.015$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" /> inches. The side of the tape is wrapped into an annulus(The shaded region between 2 circles with the same center), meaning the area of the shaded region is equal to the area of the really thin rectangle. \n</p><p>The area of the shaded region is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/2/202e364d5123dfb93f6477739cdf80756e55c1e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi(\\frac{4}{2})^2 -\\pi(\\frac{2}{2})^2 = 3\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"160\" height=\"38\" />, and we divide that by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/8/958af00c2d554d63afe1c50b8c2da32e1bd13abc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.015$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" /> to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/c/45cd4cb916f80262a8f8fa797e5ae35a2641c312.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$200\\pi$\" width=\"37\" height=\"12\" />. Approximating <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2ca003a7da0de4994b4733e203b74ff52d42553.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> to be 3, we get the final answer to be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/b/f2bac6e884037b40f9fd5d655ce5896e7b6d8b75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$200 \\cdot 3 = \\textbf {(B) } 600$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"138\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The volume of the tape is always the same, but we can either calculate it when the tape is unrolled as a really long, thin rectangular prism, or we can calculate it as a cylinder with a hole cut out of it. When we calculate it as a long rectangular prism, we can say that the length is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> (this is what the problem wants!) and the width is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. Then, the volume is, of course, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/b/d7b6d6875dbc826e1beab78176e60bc0cfdfafcd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.015 \\cdot X \\cdot Y.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"103\" height=\"14\" /> Now, notice that the \"width\" of our rectangular prism is also the \"height\" of our cylinder with a hole cut out of it. Then, we can calculate the volume as base times height, or in this case, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/7/c47493b0a403a7a738199dbc37261496c9d11266.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\pi \\cdot Y.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"14\" /> Now, since the volume always stays the same, we know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/c/a0cca9736d8a5c051b9df5a58778527e4be1b2e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\pi \\cdot Y = 0.015 \\cdot X \\cdot Y.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"175\" height=\"14\" /> Cancelling the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />'s give us an equation for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, and if we approximate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2ca003a7da0de4994b4733e203b74ff52d42553.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/7/6c7f6fa51c6040355f5703df08c50bb1c606a6f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X = \\boxed {600}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"81\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If you cannot notice that the average diameter is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />,\nyou can still solve this problem by the following method. \n</p><p>The same with solution 1, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/1/9713692589402cb5ead13603bf54f4ec81214e64.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1000}{0.015}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"47\" height=\"38\" /> layers of tape. If we consider every layers with the diameter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, the length should be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/0/190044073b533873f839b6e7d6885ed7eb9cd0c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1000}{0.015}2\\pi\\approx 400$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"119\" height=\"38\" />. If the diameter is seem as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, the length should be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/7/557a9700315ffdc6da28316197f148da6ee2a3ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$800$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" />. So, the length is between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/9/e8924412a12becf7905729885a46e3e351ec5a86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$400$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/7/557a9700315ffdc6da28316197f148da6ee2a3ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$800$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" />, the only possible answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/b/15ba7407d40b78e416405cb7cdff64554a3ba647.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{600}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"39\" height=\"23\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Daily_Dose_of_Math\">Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/OwJvuq6F7sQ\">https://youtu.be/OwJvuq6F7sQ</a>\n</p><p>~bobbert\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_DDM\">Video Solution by DDM</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by DDM\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/29xcfUcmTgg\">https://youtu.be/29xcfUcmTgg</a>\n</p><p>~piggydude\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_Math-X\">Video Solution 1 by Math-X</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by Math-X\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/BaE00H2SHQM?si=5YWwuZ_961azySZ-&amp;t=6686\">https://youtu.be/BaE00H2SHQM?si=5YWwuZ_961azySZ-&amp;t=6686</a>\n</p><p>~Math-X\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_Central_Valley_Math_Circle_.28Goes_through_full_thought_process.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solutio